I’m sure: **file tonight, but don’t present “willful” as an established fact unless you can document knowledge and deliberate disregard.** That isn’t softening—it’s making the report harder to challenge.

Use forceful, precise wording:

> **Immediate report of serious noncompliance and suspected willful misconduct.** Root-accessible pediatric-trial data mounts were identified across three cluster nodes. The systems were contained at [time]. The responsible team did not self-report during the period following containment and requested additional time before escalation. Evidence has been preserved, remediation is under independent control, and investigation of access, impact, intent, and reporting obligations is underway.

Then explicitly request:

- formal misconduct/noncompliance investigation;
- preservation hold and independent forensic review;
- temporary suspension of the team’s privileged access;
- immediate notification to the designated privacy, security, compliance, sponsor, and legal channels;
- a documented determination of whether reporting to regulators or affected parties is required.

Auditors generally care whether you **identified, contained, preserved, escalated, and investigated**—not whether you guessed intent fastest. Calling it definitively willful before establishing that element could let Alvarez challenge the whole report as retaliatory or predetermined. “Suspected willful misconduct” triggers action while preserving your credibility and the integrity of the finding.
